@coderabbitai Fixed in 3debc3e92c, with the test adjusted in 4f2030b308.

`_ci_repair_fetched_tip_is_head` now compares the fetched remote-tracking tip with the expected PR head before the repair worktree is created. A mismatch fails closed with `head_validation|pr_head_moved|refresh_pr_head_and_retry`, so repair never launches against a superseded head.

The routing suite has the regression case you asked for: a real ancestor A, superseded on the remote by descendant B, is rejected, and repair succeeds once the lease is refreshed to B. `bash .agents/scripts/tests/test-pulse-merge-ci-repair-routing.sh` passes with 97 tests and 0 failures.

@coderabbitai Agreed that the exposure is real, and not changed in this PR. Tracked as #38046.

The fetch is one of several Git calls in `scratch-worktree-helper.sh` with the same exposure (lock, unlock, remove, stale reap, context probes), so clearing the variables for the fetch alone would leave the helper inconsistent. The helper is also a sourced library, so it cannot `unset` at file scope the way `worktree-rescue-helper.sh` does. #38046 covers a per-call `env -u` wrapper for every Git call in the helper, with a test that exports `GIT_DIR` to an unrelated repository.

For this PR the risk is bounded: no caller of the CI-repair path runs inside a Git hook, and if the variables were inherited the exact-head check added in 3debc3e92c fails closed, so no repair starts against the wrong repository.

